{"version":"ai-mcq-bank-v2-la-batch15-high-quality-rewritten-affected-2026-05-22-stem-fixed-inequality-rendering-fixed-student-facing-qc-2026-05-31-pt-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch2-foundations-counting-2026-06-11-pt-batch3-counting-probability-rules-2026-06-11-pt-batch4-conditioning-2026-06-11-pt-batch5-random-variables-2026-06-11-pt-batch6-discrete-distributions-2026-06-11-pt-batch7-distribution-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch8-continuous-expectation-2026-06-11-pt-batch9-non-week7-expectation-transform-2026-06-11-pt-batch10-non-week7-process-summary-2026-06-11-pt-batch11-non-week7-final-summary-shape-2026-06-11-quality-round1-2026-06-14-quality-round2-2026-06-14-quality-round3-2026-06-14-quality-round4-deep-2026-06-14+quality-round5-difficulty-2026-06-14+quality-round6-expression-2026-06-14+quality-round7-answer-cue-2026-06-14+quality-round8-answer-shape-2026-06-14+quality-round9-answer-position-2026-06-14+quality-round10-template-padding-2026-06-14+quality-round11-generic-stems-2026-06-14+quality-round12-deterministic-cleanup-2026-06-15+quality-pilot-deep-rework-2026-06-15","concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Probability-Theory-for-EOR/inclusion-exclusion-principle.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Probability-Theory-for-EOR/inclusion-exclusion-principle.html","questions":[{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the inclusion-exclusion formula for two events $A$ and $B$?","options":["$P(A\\cup B)=P(A)P(B)$","$P(A\\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B)$","$P(A\\cap B)=P(A)+P(B)$","$P(A\\cup B)=P(A)-P(B)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7fe808bd32b0e25f4c472b2b"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What problem does inclusion-exclusion correct?","options":["Double counting of overlapping outcomes","Negative probabilities in a sample space","Ordering of selected objects","Conditioning on an impossible event"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3f4abd2def42718dd2790a8b"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_003","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For three events, $P(A\\cup B\\cup C)$ is the single-event sum minus the pairwise intersections, then:","options":["plus $P(A\\cap B\\cap C)$","minus $P(A\\cap B\\cap C)$","plus nothing","minus $P(A)P(B)P(C)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ab890fd6b8205b62736d0e55"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_004","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Inclusion-exclusion gives the bound $P(A\\cup B)\\le$:","options":["$\\min(P(A),P(B))$","$P(A)+P(B)$","$P(A)+P(B)-1$","$P(A)\\,P(B)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2b4cd8e49ba9b4b6fe0fe2b2"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Inclusion-exclusion (rather than simple addition) is needed precisely when:","options":["one event is impossible","the events are independent","the events overlap, i.e. $P(A\\cap B)>0$","the events are disjoint"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"27616a64fdef49173dd2a832"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $P(A)=0.4$, $P(B)=0.3$, and $P(A\\cap B)=0.1$, what is $P(A\\cup B)$?","options":["$0.8$","$0.6$","$0.12$","$0.1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e6c160154988225a59c4c83b"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A class has $18$ students taking mathematics, $12$ taking statistics, and $5$ taking both. How many take mathematics or statistics?","options":["$30$","$35$","$25$","$6$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bd62b64bdd7dabe0621e9a86"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"In a deck, which event requires subtracting an overlap when computing a union?","options":["Drawing a heart or a face card","Drawing a red card and a black card on one draw","Drawing no card","Drawing a card from an empty deck"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9106114c10c40f8f8a18169e"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $A$ and $B$ are disjoint with $P(A)=0.2$ and $P(B)=0.5$, what is $P(A\\cup B)$?","options":["$0.1$","$0.7$","$0.3$","$1.0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a3be6c6d39a55098fdc6e496"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=\\{1,2,3\\}$ and $B=\\{3,4\\}$, how many elements are in $A\\cup B$?","options":["$4$","$5$","$1$","$2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c49c53c92b02c73dfebfcab1"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the triple overlap added back in the three-event version?","options":["It is never counted in the first place","It is outside the sample space","It represents an independent event automatically.","It has been subtracted too many times after pairwise removal"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5cbae4822b82239d64046f50"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the most common error inclusion-exclusion prevents?","options":["Dividing by $P(B)$ when $P(B)>0$","Adding $P(A)$ and $P(B)$ without adjusting for $A\\cap B$","Treating a partition as exhaustive","Using prior probabilities in Bayes' rule"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"331e9b18f392f2d0acb3a7c9"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"When does the formula $P(A\\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)$ hold?","options":["Whenever $A$ and $B$ overlap","Only when $A=B$","When $A$ and $B$ are disjoint","Only when $P(A)=P(B)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3d3698c688bdf229f4d41e8b"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $P(A\\cup B)=0.7$, $P(A)=0.5$, and $P(B)=0.4$, what is $P(A\\cap B)$?","options":["$0.9$","$0.2$","$0.1$","$0.35$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5f6f34c4bd9549e54f20f3fb"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"In a survey, $40$ like tea, $35$ like coffee, and $20$ like both. How many like tea or coffee?","options":["$75$","$95$","$20$","$55$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9f3715912faadca08037d064"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A heart-or-king card event has $13$ hearts, $4$ kings, and $1$ king of hearts. How many favorable cards are there?","options":["$17$","$52$","$16$","$12$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"28e1ba2504252cf02f1b6ad6"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is subtracting $P(A\\cap B)$ incorrect if $A$ and $B$ are already known disjoint?","options":["It makes the probability negative in every case.","It changes the union into an intersection","It is harmless since the term is zero, but unnecessary","It assumes the events are independent"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"39623c53c757299438a10c22"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does inclusion-exclusion say about overlapping events with positive overlap?","options":["$P(A\\cup B)$ is less than $P(A)+P(B)$","$P(A\\cup B)$ equals $P(A)+P(B)$","$P(A\\cup B)$ is always zero","$P(A\\cup B)$ must equal $P(A\\cap B)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"737a4c1d1493afd5c364c963"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Three alarms have probabilities $0.2$, $0.3$, and $0.4$; pairwise overlaps are $0.05$, $0.06$, and $0.07$; the triple overlap is $0.01$. What is the inclusion-exclusion expression for at least one alarm?","options":["$0.2+0.3+0.4$","$0.2+0.3+0.4-0.05-0.06-0.07+0.01$","$0.05+0.06+0.07-0.01$.","$0.2\\cdot0.3\\cdot0.4$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e56e08484db1482f8869f96d"},{"id":"pt_batch4_inclusion_exclusion_principle_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $P(A)=0.6$, $P(B)=0.6$, and $P(A\\cap B)=0.1$, what issue appears?","options":["$P(A\\cup B)=1.1$, impossible for a probability","The events must be disjoint","The intersection should be ignored","The probabilities are valid only if $A=B$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"af67980fd07a061022520632"}]}}